{
  "document": "UC1 Stage 3 Handoff: Architecture and Design (from Technical Research, self-contained)",
  "how_to_use": "Paste this JSON into a new independent Claude conversation together with the text of stage_3_master_prompt (also supplied inside this file). No other attachment is needed.",
  "generated_on": "2026-11-13",
  "researcher_limits": "Research done in one session using web search; SAP Help Portal pages were mostly reachable only via search snippets, SAP KBA previews, SAP Learning and community mirrors, so SAP-specific design claims are labelled accordingly. No prototype or benchmark was run.",
  "stage": "Stage 2 (technical research) complete. Next: Stage 3 architecture, technical design and Claude Code implementation prompt.",
  "stage_3_master_prompt": "# MASTER PROMPT - UC1 Architecture and Design Stage (conversation 3 of 3)\n\n(Paste this prompt first, then paste the full contents of UC1_Stage3_Handoff.json in the same message or immediately after. No other attachment is needed.)\n\n## 1. Your role\nAct as SAP Commerce solution architect, Spartacus/Angular architect, AI orchestration architect, security/privacy reviewer and technical writer. Stages 1 (product discovery) and 2 (technical research) are complete and captured in the JSON. You produce the detailed architecture, technical design and the Claude Code implementation prompt. You do not write production code.\n\n## 2. Inputs and rules\n- The JSON is authoritative. product_definition carries the approved scope, 43-capability catalogue, scenarios, principles, decisions D01-D37 and open questions. coding_convention_reference, protected_decisions, research findings, feasibility, answers, spikes, risks and decisions_needing_owner_approval are included.\n- Protected decisions must not change without product-owner approval. Where your design needs to touch one, list it under owner approvals with evidence and options; never change it silently.\n- The Coding Convention Reference is mandatory. Preserve it and carry it, complete, into your output. The implementation prompt must instruct Claude Code to inspect the owner's supplied examples and follow them. Request clearer examples for anything marked unknown (examples_needed_from_owner).\n- Keep verified facts, assumptions and recommendations visibly separate. Re-verify any evidence marked 'Secondary source only' or 'Not verified' before depending on it. Provider terms and platform versions may have changed since 2026-11-13.\n- Research recommendations are proposals until the owner confirms them. Start with decisions_needing_owner_approval (A1-A14): confirm defaults in one brief round, then proceed.\n\n## 3. Mission and required outputs\n1. Architecture overview (extensions, layers, SPIs/gateways, runtime flow for streaming, orchestration, sanitisation, audit, configuration, pack switching) with diagrams.\n2. Technical design: item types and Backoffice configuration, DTO beans, conceptual-to-concrete OCC contracts (including streaming events and non-streaming fallback), tool catalogue contracts, workflow templates per intent and scenario, DAO/FlexibleSearch design following the supplied conventions, provider adapter design, sanitisation design, prompt and schema design, response contract, frontend component and state design, theming, accessibility, ImpEx/pack generator design, test strategy, deployment and configuration.\n3. Capability-to-design traceability for all 43 IDs and ten scenarios SC-01..SC-10.\n4. Spike plan (S1-S8) with pass/fail criteria and the design branches each outcome triggers.\n5. The Claude Code implementation prompt: staged build plan, repository layout, step-by-step prompts, acceptance checks, and the explicit instruction to inspect the supplied examples and follow the conventions.\n6. Open items and owner approvals still outstanding.\n\n## 4. Method\nWork section by section; ask the product owner one focused question at a time only when a decision is genuinely theirs; otherwise recommend a default and continue. Search current documentation for any API, version or provider term you rely on and label it Verified, Assumption or Recommendation with source and date.\n\n## 5. Final instruction\nBe rigorous and honest, keep enterprise execution secure and controlled, and keep AI as interpreter and communicator with SAP Commerce Java as the executor of record.\n",
  "claude_code_instruction": "Claude Code MUST inspect the owner-supplied example code and follow the Coding Convention Reference below before writing any code. Patterns are binding; class names and the fpp/Cpp prefixes are placeholders. Where a convention is marked 'Not visible / unknown', stop and request the example (list in examples_needed_from_owner) rather than inventing a style. Mark future-scope extension points with comments (unit-level access, live stock, real cart, carrier tracking).",
  "coding_convention_reference": {
    "provenance_note": "Source: owner-supplied screenshots of sample code (a chat/controller/facade/service example plus a DAO example). The owner has stated they are sample code but correct. Patterns are binding; specific class names and the fpp/Cpp prefixes are placeholders and must not be assumed for the accelerator. Several lines are truncated in the screenshots; request a clearer example before relying on those details.",
    "mandatory_instruction": "Every later stage must follow these conventions. The final implementation prompt must instruct Claude Code to inspect the supplied examples and follow them. Request clearer examples for anything marked unknown.",
    "classification_legend": {
      "Confirmed from screenshots": "directly visible",
      "Likely convention requiring validation": "inferred",
      "Not visible / unknown": "not seen"
    },
    "conventions": [
      {
        "area": "Package naming",
        "observation": "Base prefix com.fpp. DTO beans: com.fpp.facades.data.chat.* (facade DTOs) and com.fpp.occ.chat.* (OCC ws DTOs).",
        "status": "Confirmed from screenshots"
      },
      {
        "area": "Class naming",
        "observation": "Interface + Default implementation: ChatFacade/DefaultChatFacade, ChatService/DefaultChatService; DAO interface + DaoImpl (CppDeliveryIssueDao/CppDeliveryIssueDaoImpl). Facade DTOs end in Data; OCC DTOs end in WsDTO.",
        "status": "Confirmed from screenshots"
      },
      {
        "area": "Prefix usage",
        "observation": "'fpp' appears on packages and Spring bean names; 'Cpp' appears on a DAO/model example. Whether these are two intentional prefixes is not known.",
        "status": "Likely convention requiring validation"
      },
      {
        "area": "DTO definition",
        "observation": "DTOs declared in XML beans files: facade-beans.xml for *Data and occ-beans.xml for *WsDTO, with property name and type (e.g. java.lang.String).",
        "status": "Confirmed from screenshots"
      },
      {
